# Line endings, so an agent's editor cannot decide them.
#
# Added 2026-09-08 after a session committed four files as CRLF with stray
# lone CRs in them. That combination is the dangerous one: git's automatic
# text/binary detection treats a lone CR as a sign of a binary file and then
# skips end-of-line normalisation entirely, so the CRLFs went into the index
# verbatim. The result was 7,672 insertions for 264 lines of work, `git blame`
# on WINDOWS-HANDOFF.md attributing all 6,655 lines to one commit, and a diff
# no reviewer could read. It was caught by review; nothing enforced it.
#
# This repository is LF: measured on the day this file was written,
# `git ls-files --eol` reported 13,091 tracked files as i/lf, 138 detected as
# binary, and exactly two not LF - both vendored, both excluded below.

# The default. Git decides text from binary and stores text as LF.
* text=auto

# Explicitly text, which is the part that matters. An explicit `text` skips
# the content heuristic, so a file that has picked up a stray CR is still
# normalised instead of being mistaken for binary. `text=auto` alone would NOT
# have prevented the failure above.
#
# What this file does NOT do, measured rather than assumed: git strips only the
# CR directly before each newline, so a file written as CR CR LF still lands in
# the index with one CR per line -- which is the exact shape that caused the
# damage. `.githooks/pre-commit` checks the staged blobs for that and says so.
# This file closes ordinary CRLF drift; the hook closes the rest, and hooks
# reach a clone only after `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`.
#
# No `eol=` on any of these on purpose: the working tree keeps whatever the
# platform gives it (CRLF on Windows via core.autocrlf), so adding this file
# changes nothing anyone has checked out. Only what reaches the index is fixed.
*.md      text
*.txt     text
*.json    text
*.yml     text
*.yaml    text
*.cs      text
*.csproj  text
*.sln     text
*.xaml    text
*.swift   text
*.py      text
*.sh      text
*.ps1     text
*.bat     text
*.cmd     text
*.js      text
*.ts      text
*.css     text
*.scss    text
*.html    text

# Vendored, and kept byte-for-byte as upstream ships it: the only two tracked
# files that are not LF, one CRLF and one mixed. Normalising them would rewrite
# a third party's file and produce a 2,150-line diff that says nothing.
#
# Named one by one, NOT as `.obsidian/**`. The wider pattern was written first
# and checked before committing: it also caught app.json, appearance.json,
# core-plugins.json and workspace.json, which ARE LF in the index and merely
# CRLF in a Windows working tree - and `-text` on those makes git compare the
# two literally, so `git add --renormalize` staged their CRLF. The exclusion
# would have committed the exact damage this file exists to prevent.
support/obsidian_defaults/.obsidian/themes/Minimal/manifest.json -text
support/obsidian_defaults/.obsidian/themes/Minimal/theme.css     -text
